That still goes on even when
you become a professional

00:09:29.540 --> 00:09:31.910
and you're doing competitions,
still the same problem.

00:09:31.910 --> 00:09:32.540
MARK MANDEL: Still
the same problem.

00:09:32.540 --> 00:09:34.290
WENDY KAN: It is very
exciting, because it

00:09:34.290 --> 00:09:38.330
is the machine learning 101 or
stats 101 problem that people

00:09:38.330 --> 00:09:39.050
want to solve.

00:09:39.050 --> 00:09:41.450
We actually had to jump
through a lot of hoops,

00:09:41.450 --> 00:09:44.531
in terms of getting the data and
having the license to release

00:09:44.531 --> 00:09:45.030
the data.

00:09:45.030 --> 00:09:47.630
So it's not that easy
to get it running,

00:09:47.630 --> 00:09:48.800
but it's very exciting.

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:51.290
And we feel like our
community will love it a lot.

00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:51.710
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:09:51.710 --> 00:09:53.960
MARK MANDEL: Cool, so from
a developer perspective--

00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:55.946
I want to go do
this competition.

00:09:55.946 --> 00:09:57.320
What's my process
for doing that?

00:09:57.320 --> 00:09:58.700
And then, what's my end result?

00:09:58.700 --> 00:10:00.927
How do you determine
whether I win or I lose?

00:10:00.927 --> 00:10:02.760
WENDY KAN: For a normal
Kaggle competition--

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:05.574
the Zillow one is a
particular example,

00:10:05.574 --> 00:10:06.740
and I'll go into that later.

00:10:06.740 --> 00:10:08.970
But for a normal
Kaggle competition,

00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:11.790
you download the data
from a Kaggle site,

00:10:11.790 --> 00:10:14.520
or you don't download the
data and use Kaggle Kernels

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:19.170
and just do online analysis and
use your browser and everything

00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:20.640
is run on the Cloud.

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:23.550
And then you can basically
read through the documentation

00:10:23.550 --> 00:10:27.390
that we provide, or just
have fun and explore the data

00:10:27.390 --> 00:10:29.010
itself on the Cloud.

00:10:29.010 --> 00:10:32.760
For the competitions, there's
always a column or property

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:35.130
that you are going to
predict, and there is always

00:10:35.130 --> 00:10:37.260
going to be a metric
that calculates

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:39.810
how well you're predicting
against the ground

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:41.370
truth-- the answer.

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:46.890
And you basically submit a bunch
of predictions in the CSV file,

00:10:46.890 --> 00:10:48.960
and we'll give you a score.

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:50.820
And that score puts
you on the leaderboard,

00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:53.340
and you can see that there are
a lot of people ahead of you.

00:10:53.340 --> 00:10:55.950
You will want to
improve your score

00:10:55.950 --> 00:10:58.350
and beat these people
in front of you.

00:10:58.350 --> 00:11:01.680
One thing to note is that to do
good machine learning practice,

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:04.010
we have a public and
private leaderboard

00:11:04.010 --> 00:11:06.090
to prevent people
from overfitting.

00:11:06.090 --> 00:11:08.190
Because when you
keep predicting,

00:11:08.190 --> 00:11:10.770
you're going to overfit your
model, even if you're not

00:11:10.770 --> 00:11:13.320
trying to just cheat.

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:16.560
Your model will overfit
to the ground truth.

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:20.340
So we ask people to predict
a bunch of predictions,

00:11:20.340 --> 00:11:23.570
and only some of that go
into the public leaderboard

00:11:23.570 --> 00:11:26.550
that people can get feedback
on and instantly improve

00:11:26.550 --> 00:11:27.420
their models.

00:11:27.420 --> 00:11:29.520
But the final results
are determined

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:31.530
from a private
leaderboard where people

00:11:31.530 --> 00:11:34.290
don't get the score right
away, and that's revealed

00:11:34.290 --> 00:11:35.765
at the end of a competition.

00:11:35.765 --> 00:11:38.490
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool, so that's
like when you're doing machine

00:11:38.490 --> 00:11:42.220
learning, you have the
training set and then the test

00:11:42.220 --> 00:11:43.730
set and the validation set--

00:11:43.730 --> 00:11:44.500
WENDY KAN: That's exactly it.

00:11:44.500 --> 00:11:45.420
FRANCESC CAMPOY: You're
actually exposing

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:47.730
the training and the test,
but not the validation.

00:11:47.730 --> 00:11:49.020
WENDY KAN: Exactly.

00:11:49.020 --> 00:11:51.690
We give people the
training, and the validation

00:11:51.690 --> 00:11:54.080
is equivalent to the
public leaderboard

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:55.980
and the holdout
set is equivalent

00:11:55.980 --> 00:11:57.320
to the private leaderboard.

00:11:57.320 --> 00:11:58.020
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK, cool.

00:11:58.020 --> 00:11:58.680
MARK MANDEL: And
for those people

00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:01.110
who don't know what overfitting
is, what's overfitting?

00:12:01.110 --> 00:12:03.360
WENDY KAN: That's when your
model is learning things

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:05.400
that it's not supposed to know.

00:12:05.400 --> 00:12:09.360
So basically, if there's
particular things in this data

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:12.000
set that is not representative
of the population,

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:14.490
and your model
basically overfits

00:12:14.490 --> 00:12:19.864
to the characteristics of this
particular sample of the data.

00:12:19.864 --> 00:12:20.780
And that's an overfit.

00:12:20.780 --> 00:12:21.870
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
The way I learned

00:12:21.870 --> 00:12:23.453
that-- which, I think
is pretty cool--

00:12:23.453 --> 00:12:25.390
is imagine that you
have a bunch of points.

00:12:25.390 --> 00:12:28.710
If you have more
parameters than points,

00:12:28.710 --> 00:12:30.420
you can actually
draw a line that

00:12:30.420 --> 00:12:33.360
goes every single one
of the points, which

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:35.050
doesn't make any sense.

00:12:35.050 --> 00:12:37.075
But has 100% accuracy
for the data.

00:12:37.075 --> 00:12:38.700
MARK MANDEL: For
exactly that data set.

00:12:38.700 --> 00:12:40.020
FRANCESC CAMPOY: But if
you give any other data,

00:12:40.020 --> 00:12:41.070
it would be completely wrong.

00:12:41.070 --> 00:12:41.800
MARK MANDEL: Got it.

00:12:41.800 --> 00:12:42.180
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:12:42.180 --> 00:12:44.970
WENDY KAN: If you use a house
price prediction example,

00:12:44.970 --> 00:12:50.460
it'll be like, oh, this house
is $3 million and it has--

00:12:50.460 --> 00:12:53.430
I don't know-- a
door that's broken.

00:12:53.430 --> 00:12:56.100
And so your model learns
that, and then predicts

00:12:56.100 --> 00:12:59.580
if you have a broken door,
then it's $3 million,

00:12:59.580 --> 00:13:01.840
and that's not the case
for all the houses.

00:13:01.840 --> 00:13:04.724
But that's unfortunately
the sample that you pick.

00:13:04.724 --> 00:13:07.140
MARK MANDEL: All right, and
two-- outside of competitions,

00:13:07.140 --> 00:13:08.760
you also host data sets?

00:13:08.760 --> 00:13:12.830
Is that together or separate, or
what's the relationship there?

00:13:12.830 --> 00:13:15.480
It It is a separate
offering, and that part

00:13:15.480 --> 00:13:17.160
is relatively new on Kaggle.

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:20.010
So we host open
data sets on Kaggle,

00:13:20.010 --> 00:13:22.200
because we feel like
Kaggle Kernels is a very

00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:23.490
strong tool for people to use.

00:13:23.490 --> 00:13:26.140
And hosting these
public data sets

00:13:26.140 --> 00:13:28.820
lets people explore
these data sets

00:13:28.820 --> 00:13:30.630
and share their
findings on Kaggle.

00:13:30.630 --> 00:13:35.070
And also, it's just
for public good.

00:13:35.070 --> 00:13:38.190
We've all been there
to look for a data set,

00:13:38.190 --> 00:13:40.725
and you're just randomly
searching on the internet,

00:13:40.725 --> 00:13:44.470
and then you feel like that's
available and you can't--

00:13:44.470 --> 00:13:46.620
it's a good place to find
all sorts of data sets.

00:13:46.620 --> 00:13:48.720
And now it's pretty organic.

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:51.390
So far, we publish some,
and then the community

00:13:51.390 --> 00:13:52.330
will publish some.

00:13:52.330 --> 00:13:55.090
And a lot of times, we're
