00:14:49.110 --> 00:14:50.917
talk about exactly
what that is, because I

00:14:50.917 --> 00:14:53.500
think it's actually super, super
cool when I had a look at it.

00:14:53.500 --> 00:14:55.140
WENDY KAN: So
Kaggle Kernels has--

00:14:55.140 --> 00:14:57.660
I think we launched it
maybe two years ago?

00:14:57.660 --> 00:15:00.252
You go to Kaggle.com/Kernels.

00:15:00.252 --> 00:15:00.960
It's very simple.

00:15:00.960 --> 00:15:03.690
It takes care of
basically everything

00:15:03.690 --> 00:15:08.420
that a data scientist
needs for very quickly run

00:15:08.420 --> 00:15:10.140
some code to analyze data.

00:15:10.140 --> 00:15:13.880
So you click open a
notebook, or open a code,

00:15:13.880 --> 00:15:15.800
and it pops up in
your browser, and you

00:15:15.800 --> 00:15:16.940
start editing your code.

00:15:16.940 --> 00:15:19.570
And the data is
there depending on--

00:15:19.570 --> 00:15:22.110
if you're in a competition,
then the competition's data

00:15:22.110 --> 00:15:23.000
is all in there.

00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:27.020
If you're in a data set, the
data set's data's all there.

00:15:27.020 --> 00:15:32.030
So as a data scientist, before,
a lot of times you go in

00:15:32.030 --> 00:15:35.510
and you receive a new data set,
and you want to explore it.

00:15:35.510 --> 00:15:38.870
If you don't have a computer
