00:15:42.437 --> 00:15:44.270
then you're going to
spend hours doing that.

00:15:44.270 --> 00:15:49.700
But for this one, also
uses a Docker image,

00:15:49.700 --> 00:15:51.260
and that's community maintained.

00:15:51.260 --> 00:15:54.050
So most of the packages
that people want to use

00:15:54.050 --> 00:15:55.620
are already in there.

00:15:55.620 --> 00:15:57.320
So it's pretty convenient.

00:15:57.320 --> 00:16:00.200
It's also a great place
to show off your work

00:16:00.200 --> 00:16:02.780
and to benefit from
other people's work.

00:16:02.780 --> 00:16:06.950
Usually when you run it,
it already becomes public,

00:16:06.950 --> 00:16:08.390
so people can see it.

00:16:08.390 --> 00:16:13.340
So if you want to say, I
have this new text data set

00:16:13.340 --> 00:16:16.220
and I want to see what
other people do with it,

00:16:16.220 --> 00:16:18.179
and then you read other
people's notebook.

00:16:18.179 --> 00:16:19.970
And they already did
this amazing analysis.

00:16:19.970 --> 00:16:22.261
And you can be like, oh, I
have this new idea that they

00:16:22.261 --> 00:16:23.270
can extend from.

00:16:23.270 --> 00:16:26.250
Then it's just like GitHub
where you can fork it,

00:16:26.250 --> 00:16:28.510
and then you can
make a new version.

00:16:28.510 --> 00:16:30.800
With that, you still don't
have to download any code

00:16:30.800 --> 00:16:31.640
on your computer.

00:16:31.640 --> 00:16:33.560
It's just all
stays on the Cloud,

00:16:33.560 --> 00:16:35.594
