00:29:26.656 --> 00:29:28.530
The question of the
week, I'm going to ask it

00:29:28.530 --> 00:29:30.200
because the answer is--

00:29:30.200 --> 00:29:33.390
I think you're going to
probably give it better than me.

00:29:33.390 --> 00:29:37.430
But it's-- OK, I want to do
client side load balancing with

00:29:37.430 --> 00:29:38.870
gRPC and Kubernetes.

00:29:38.870 --> 00:29:41.380
So if you have a
Kubernetes service,

00:29:41.380 --> 00:29:44.090
what you get from
the service normally

00:29:44.090 --> 00:29:47.750
is one IP, which is virtual
IP, that you send your requests

00:29:47.750 --> 00:29:49.790
there and it's done.

00:29:49.790 --> 00:29:52.070
Somehow it gets load
balanced to a bunch

00:29:52.070 --> 00:29:53.817
of pods that are behind.

00:29:53.817 --> 00:29:55.400
But the problem is
that as long as you

00:29:55.400 --> 00:29:57.320
keep one connection
with gRPC, you

00:29:57.320 --> 00:30:00.410
will send all of your requests
all the time to the same pod,

00:30:00.410 --> 00:30:01.760
until that pod restarts.

00:30:01.760 --> 00:30:03.680
And then you will send
it to someone else.

00:30:03.680 --> 00:30:07.040
But what if I want to actually
distribute that load across all

00:30:07.040 --> 00:30:08.340
of the pods that are available?

00:30:08.340 --> 00:30:10.560
How do I do that
from the client?

00:30:10.560 --> 00:30:12.742
MARK MANDEL: So yeah,
this is a fun problem,

00:30:12.742 --> 00:30:14.950
and something that's been
talked about a lot in gRPC.

00:30:14.950 --> 00:30:16.783
I'm sure you can find
lots of threads on it.

00:30:16.783 --> 00:30:19.850
The basic answer for
gRPC is-- what you often

00:30:19.850 --> 00:30:22.370
do is what's called client
side load balancing.

00:30:22.370 --> 00:30:25.790
So basically, you feed
a series of IP addresses

00:30:25.790 --> 00:30:27.627
to your gRPC client,
then you tell it

00:30:27.627 --> 00:30:28.960
how you want to load balance it.

00:30:28.960 --> 00:30:30.170
So Round-robin, right?

00:30:30.170 --> 00:30:31.590
Does one then the other, and
then the other, then the other,

00:30:31.590 --> 00:30:32.756
then goes back around again.

00:30:32.756 --> 00:30:34.340
So the fun question
becomes, how do I

00:30:34.340 --> 00:30:35.464
get all those IP addresses?

00:30:35.464 --> 00:30:38.360
How do I tell it the 4, 5,
15 IP addresses and ports

00:30:38.360 --> 00:30:39.890
that it needs to
know to connect to

00:30:39.890 --> 00:30:42.590
and then can then subsequently
send and distribute

00:30:42.590 --> 00:30:44.397
those requests across
those IP addresses?

00:30:44.397 --> 00:30:45.980
So inside Kubernetes,
there's a couple

00:30:45.980 --> 00:30:47.390
of nice ways of doing it.

00:30:47.390 --> 00:30:51.080
We'll link in the show
notes to a GitHub repository

00:30:51.080 --> 00:30:53.650
that one of our other
teammates [INAUDIBLE] started.

00:30:53.650 --> 00:30:55.819
This is a Java example,
because I know Francesc

00:30:55.819 --> 00:30:57.110
is all about that all the time.

00:30:57.110 --> 00:30:59.090
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I love Java.

00:30:59.090 --> 00:30:59.970
MARK MANDEL: Exactly.

00:30:59.970 --> 00:31:01.136
We chose two different ways.

00:31:01.136 --> 00:31:04.160
So one way is using
DNS service discovery,

00:31:04.160 --> 00:31:06.920
which is a built-in tool
kit that comes with gRPC.

00:31:06.920 --> 00:31:09.280
So-- last week, I want to
say, or several weeks ago,

00:31:09.280 --> 00:31:10.220
I can't remember now--

00:31:10.220 --> 00:31:12.230
we talked about headless
services in Kubernetes.

00:31:12.230 --> 00:31:16.430
So what those end up being is
rather than having a single DNS

00:31:16.430 --> 00:31:19.650
entry that does a load balance
itself, like a server-side load

00:31:19.650 --> 00:31:22.440
balancer that points out to all
these pods behind it, what it

00:31:22.440 --> 00:31:26.437
actually has is a DNS entry but
with multiple A records, each

00:31:26.437 --> 00:31:28.020
with an A record
that points to the IP

00:31:28.020 --> 00:31:30.900
address of the various
running instances behind it.

00:31:30.900 --> 00:31:32.445
So basically, you
can hit that DNS

00:31:32.445 --> 00:31:34.320
and be like, hey, give
me all your A records,

00:31:34.320 --> 00:31:37.260
and that gives you the catalog
of all the pods behind so you

00:31:37.260 --> 00:31:39.760
can get access to all of
those running instances

00:31:39.760 --> 00:31:41.760
and know their IP addresses
and connect to them.

00:31:41.760 --> 00:31:44.040
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
then, if I am the client,

00:31:44.040 --> 00:31:47.220
then I get the list and I can
go over all of the IP addresses

00:31:47.220 --> 00:31:47.790
in that list.

00:31:47.790 --> 00:31:49.357
But what if I have many clients?

00:31:49.357 --> 00:31:50.940
MARK MANDEL: Do you
have many clients?

00:31:50.940 --> 00:31:53.130
That's fine, you can do
that for every client that

00:31:53.130 --> 00:31:54.000
ends up connecting.

00:31:54.000 --> 00:31:54.890
That's totally cool.

00:31:54.890 --> 00:31:55.230
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:31:55.230 --> 00:31:57.900
MARK MANDEL: And what's great
is gRPC comes with a DNS name

00:31:57.900 --> 00:32:01.260
resolver middleware that you can
just drop in that understands,

00:32:01.260 --> 00:32:03.930
oh, if you have multiple A
records on this DNS record,

00:32:03.930 --> 00:32:05.670
then I know that I should be
Round-robin-ing around those.

00:32:05.670 --> 00:32:07.140
So I should be load
balancing across those

00:32:07.140 --> 00:32:08.430
given a certain strategy.

00:32:08.430 --> 00:32:10.790
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
and on top of that, also--

00:32:10.790 --> 00:32:15.234
Kubernetes will rotate the order
of the IPs every single time

00:32:15.234 --> 00:32:15.900
you get the DNS.

00:32:15.900 --> 00:32:18.300
So if you get a bunch of
different clients connecting

00:32:18.300 --> 00:32:21.454
to the DNS, you will
actually end up hitting--

00:32:21.454 --> 00:32:23.870
by default-- to some kind of
Round-robin [INAUDIBLE] list,

00:32:23.870 --> 00:32:25.669
some kind of load
balancing, automatically,

00:32:25.669 --> 00:32:27.960
on top of the load balancing
that you might want to do,

00:32:27.960 --> 00:32:28.770
which is pretty cool.

00:32:28.770 --> 00:32:29.460
MARK MANDEL: That
is really cool.

00:32:29.460 --> 00:32:31.293
Now, there's another
way you can also do it,

00:32:31.293 --> 00:32:32.390
which is neat as well.

00:32:32.390 --> 00:32:35.250
And Ray gives us another
example of that, wherein

00:32:35.250 --> 00:32:38.580
you can query the Kubernetes
API service discovery-- so it's

00:32:38.580 --> 00:32:41.050
got a whole API in there
where it's like, hey,

00:32:41.050 --> 00:32:43.320
would you like to tell
me all the IP addresses?

00:32:43.320 --> 00:32:44.820
So rather than
going through DNS,

00:32:44.820 --> 00:32:46.090
you can do it through
that way as well.

00:32:46.090 --> 00:32:47.631
So there's a couple
of different ways

00:32:47.631 --> 00:32:49.350
you can do it as you
need to, depending

00:32:49.350 --> 00:32:51.160
on your wants and/or needs.

00:32:51.160 --> 00:32:54.510
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, I wrote a
little code doing exactly that,

00:32:54.510 --> 00:32:55.740
using the API--

00:32:55.740 --> 00:32:56.620
the Kubernetes API.

00:32:56.620 --> 00:32:58.740
But rather than querying
the Kubernetes API

00:32:58.740 --> 00:33:01.080
every time, what I did was
I had a little side guard

00:33:01.080 --> 00:33:03.660
that actually got notifications
from the Kubernetes API,

00:33:03.660 --> 00:33:05.114
because you can watch something.

00:33:05.114 --> 00:33:07.530
So you watch the list of IPs
and every single time there's

00:33:07.530 --> 00:33:09.420
a new pod or a pod
that disappears,

00:33:09.420 --> 00:33:11.726
you are automatically notified.

00:33:11.726 --> 00:33:13.350
So whenever you want
to send a request,

00:33:13.350 --> 00:33:15.920
you already have the list,
which makes it faster.

00:33:15.920 --> 00:33:19.560
So yeah, lots of really cool
ways to do the same thing.

00:33:19.560 --> 00:33:22.061
Really, go with the one that
works better for your workflow.

00:33:22.061 --> 00:33:23.060
MARK MANDEL: Absolutely.

